From: Cromite <cromite@cromite.org>
Date: Sat, 31 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: Add headless automation mode

Adds an on-device automation harness for Android, layered on the existing
cromite test-support private API.

When the "headless-automation-mode" cromite flag is enabled, a harness UI is
placed on top of a tab's web contents. The overlay is no longer an opaque black
view: it is a FrameLayout hosting a WebView that loads the bundled page
file:///android_asset/cromite_automation/harness.html. The harness owns all
flow logic in JavaScript (the flow registry, the run loop, and flow
add/remove/update) and talks to native automation primitives exposed as
window.automationNative:

    navigate(url), waitForSelector(sel, timeout), evaluate(js), tap(sel),
    type(sel, text), scrollTo(sel), sleep(ms), showBrowser(), hideBrowser(),
    cancel()

Interaction is human-like: JavaScript is used only to locate elements and read
results, while taps and keystrokes are dispatched as real Android input events
(MotionEvent / KeyEvent) through the container view, with randomized timing and
small coordinate jitter, so the renderer sees trusted events through the normal
input pipeline. The harness UI ships two tabs (a UI-only Chat tab and a Flows
tab that lists flows with an add button); the renderer keeps compositing while
the overlay is shown, so automation continues to run while the page is hidden.

The previously added window.cromite.setContentVisible / isContentVisible methods
remain for optional external (WebDriver/Appium) control of the same overlay.

Build-environment integration (intentionally kept out of the diff, like the
generate_jni note, because it is version/tree specific):
  * Register the new Java classes (CromiteHeadlessBridge, AutomationBridge,
    AutomationEngine) with the chrome generate_jni target so the generated
    CromiteHeadlessBridge_jni.h is available to chrome/browser.
  * Add an android_assets() target that bundles
    chrome/android/java/assets/cromite_automation/harness.html into the APK at
    assets/cromite_automation/harness.html (mirror
    Experimental-user-scripts-support.patch).
  * Call CromiteHeadlessBridge.attachHarness(tab.getWebContents()) from the
    active-tab / activity init path so the harness is shown on startup when the
    flag is enabled (re-assert on active-tab change so a new tab cannot leak
    content).
  * Confirm the real-input dispatch path (container dispatchTouchEvent /
    dispatchKeyEvent vs WebContents.getEventForwarder()) for the targeted
    Chromium version.

License: GPL-3.0-only - https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-3.0-only.html
